# Colle, Lemma 3.5

Source: Cleber Fernando Colle, *On periodic decompositions, one-sided nonexpansive
directions and Nivat's conjecture*, **arXiv:1909.08195v4** (the v4 numbering is the one
used throughout; v1–v3 number things differently).  Text below is transcribed verbatim
from the ar5iv rendering `https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1909.08195`, with the LaTeX
alt-text kept as-is.

## The source text, verbatim

### Notation 1.4 (half plane), Definition 1.5, Notation 1.6

> Let $\boldsymbol{\ell}\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}$ be an oriented line. The half plane determined
> by $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ is defined as
>
> $\mathcal{H}(\boldsymbol{\ell}):=\{g\in\mathbb{Z}^{2}:\langle g,(-u_{2},u_{1})\rangle\geq 0\},$
>
> where $(u_{1},u_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{2}$ is a non-zero vector parallel to
> $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ and $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$ denotes the standard inner product of
> $\mathbb{R}^{2}$ . We remark that, following the orientation of $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ , the
> interior of $\mathcal{H}(\boldsymbol{\ell})$ is on the left.
>
> **Definition 1.5.** Let $X\subset\mathcal{A}^{\mathbb{Z}^{2}}$ be a subshift. An oriented
> line $\boldsymbol{\ell}\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}$ through the origin is called a one-sided
> expansive direction on $X$ if
>
> $\forall\ x,y\in X,\quad x|\mathcal{H}(\boldsymbol{\ell})=y|\mathcal{H}(\boldsymbol{\ell})
> \ \Longrightarrow\ x=y.$
>
> Otherwise, the oriented line $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ is called a one-sided nonexpansive
> direction on $X$ .
>
> **Notation 1.6.** Given a configuration $\eta\in\mathcal{A}^{\mathbb{Z}^{2}}$ , we use
> $\nexpd(\eta)$ to denote the set formed by the oriented lines through the origin that are
> one-sided nonexpansive directions on $X_{\eta}$ .

### Definition 2.2 (generating set)

> Let $\eta\in\mathcal{A}^{\mathbb{Z}^{2}}$ be a configuration and suppose
> $\mathcal{S}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ is a finite set. A point $g\in\mathcal{S}$ is said to be
> $\eta$ -generated by $\mathcal{S}$ if $P_{\eta}(\mathcal{S})=P_{\eta}(\mathcal{S}\backslash
> \{g\})$ . A non-empty, finite, convex subset of $\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ for which each vertex is
> $\eta$ -generated is called an $\eta$ -generating set.

### Definition 2.11 (periodicity of a restriction)

> Let $\eta\in\mathcal{A}^{\mathbb{Z}^{2}}$ be a configuration and suppose
> $\mathcal{U}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ is a non-empty set. We say that
> $\eta|\mathcal{U}$ , the restriction of $\eta$ to $\mathcal{U}$ , is periodic if there
> exists a vector $h\in(\mathbb{Z}^{2})^{*}$ , called period of $\eta|\mathcal{U}$ , such
> that $\eta_{g+h}=\eta_{g}$ for all $g\in\mathcal{U}\cap(\mathcal{U}-h)$ . If
> $\eta|\mathcal{U}$ has two periods linearly independents over $\mathbb{R}^{2}$ , we say
> that $\eta|\mathcal{U}$ is fully periodic.

### Definitions 3.1, 3.2, Notation 3.3, Definition 3.4

> **Definition 3.1.** Let $\boldsymbol{\ell},\boldsymbol{\ell}^{\prime}\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}$
> be rational oriented lines in distinct directions. A convex set $\mathcal{R}\subset
> \mathbb{Z}^{2}$ (positively oriented) with two semi-infinite edges parallels to
> $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ and $\boldsymbol{\ell}^{\prime}$ , respectively, is called an
> $(\boldsymbol{\ell},\boldsymbol{\ell}^{\prime})$ -region if
> $w\prec\cdots\prec w^{\prime}$ , where $w\in E(\mathcal{R})$ is the edge parallel to
> $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ and $w^{\prime}\in E(\mathcal{R})$ is the edge parallel to
> $\boldsymbol{\ell}^{\prime}$ .
>
> **Definition 3.2.** Let $\mathcal{U}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ be a finite, convex set such
> that $\conv(\mathcal{U})$ has positive area. A convex set $\mathcal{T}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}$
> is said to be weakly $E(\mathcal{U})$ -enveloped if, for every edge
> $\varpi\in E(\mathcal{T})$ , there exists an edge $w\in E(\mathcal{U})$ parallel to
> $\varpi$ with $|w\cap\mathcal{U}|\leq|\varpi\cap\mathcal{T}|$ . A set
> $\mathcal{T}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ weakly $E(\mathcal{U})$ -enveloped where
> $|E(\mathcal{T})|=|E(\mathcal{U})|$ is said to be $E(\mathcal{U})$ -enveloped.
>
> **Notation 3.3.** Let $\boldsymbol{\ell}\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}$ be a rational oriented line
> with $\boldsymbol{\ell}\cap\mathbb{Z}^{2}\neq\emptyset$ . We use
> $\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(-)}$ to denote the oriented line
> $\boldsymbol{\ell}^{\prime}\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}$ parallel to $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ closest
> of $\mathcal{H}(\boldsymbol{\ell})$ such that
> $\boldsymbol{\ell}^{\prime}\cap\mathbb{Z}^{2}\neq\emptyset$ and
> $\mathcal{H}(\boldsymbol{\ell})\cap\boldsymbol{\ell}^{\prime}=\emptyset$ .
>
> **Definition 3.4.** Let $\boldsymbol{\ell}\subset\mathbb{R}^{2}$ be a rational oriented
> line and suppose $B\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ is a non-empty, finite, convex set. The
> half-strip from $B$ in the direction of $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ is defined as
>
> $H_{B}(\boldsymbol{\ell}):=\{g+t\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}\in\mathbb{Z}^{2}:g\in B,\
> t\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}\}.$

### Lemma 3.5 (statement)

> In the next lemma, $(\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota})_{B^{\prime}}=(\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota})_{B}$
> means that the support lines of $B^{\prime}$ and $B$ determined by
> $\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota}$ coincide.
>
> **Lemma 3.5.** Let $\eta\in\mathcal{A}^{\mathbb{Z}^{2}}$ be a configuration with
> $\varphi(X)=(X^{h_{1}}-1)\cdots(X^{h_{m}}-1)\in\ann_{R}(\eta)$ , where
> $h_{1},\ldots,h_{m}\in\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ , with $m\geq 2$ , are vectors in pairwise distinct
> directions. Suppose $\boldsymbol{\ell}_{1},\ldots,\boldsymbol{\ell}_{2m}\subset
> \mathbb{R}^{2}$ is an enumeration of the oriented lines through the origin parallels to the
> edges of $\mathcal{S}_{\varphi}$ where the edge parallel to $\boldsymbol{\ell}_{i+1}$ is a
> successor of the edge parallel to $\boldsymbol{\ell}_{i}$ and indices are taken modulo
> $2m$ . Given $1\leq\iota\leq 2m$ , suppose $x_{per}\in X_{\eta}$ is a periodic
> configuration with period parallel to $\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota}$ .
>
> (i) If for each $E(\mathcal{S}_{\varphi})$ -enveloped set $B^{\prime}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}$
> there exist an $E(\mathcal{S}_{\varphi})$ -enveloped set $B\supset B^{\prime}$ , with
> $(\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota})_{B^{\prime}}=(\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota})_{B}$ , and
> $u\in\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ such that
>
> $(T^{u}\eta)|B=x_{per}|B,\ \ \text{but}\ \ (T^{u}\eta)|H_{B}(\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota})
> \neq x_{per}|H_{B}(\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota}),$
>
> then there exist an $(\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota},\boldsymbol{\ell}_{J})$ -region
> $\hat{A}_{\infty}$ , with $\iota+1\leq J\leq\iota+m-1$ , a configuration
> $\vartheta\in X_{\eta}$ and $\epsilon\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}$ such that
>
> (3.1) $\vartheta|\hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon)}=\hat{x}_{per}|
> \hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon)},\ \ \textrm{but}\ \ \vartheta|
> \hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon+1)}\neq\hat{x}_{per}|\hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon+1)},$
>
> where $\hat{x}_{per}:=T^{k\vec{v}_{\ell}}x_{per}$ for some $k\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}$ , for each
> $\epsilon\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}$ ,
>
> $\hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon)}:=\{g+t\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}_{J-1}}:g\in
> \hat{A}_{\infty},\ t\in\mathbb{Z}_{+},\ \dist(g+t\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}_{J-1}},
> \boldsymbol{\ell}_{J})\leq d_{\epsilon}\}$
>
> is an $(\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota},\boldsymbol{\ell}_{J})$ -region and
> $0=d_{0}<d_{1}<\cdots<d_{n}<\cdots$ is the sequence where, for each
> $g\in\mathcal{H}(\boldsymbol{\ell}_{J})$ , there exists $i\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}$ such that
> $\dist(g,\boldsymbol{\ell}_{J})=d_{i}$ .
>
> (ii) If for each $E(\mathcal{S}_{\varphi})$ -enveloped set $B^{\prime}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}$
> there exist an $E(\mathcal{S}_{\varphi})$ -enveloped set $B\supset B^{\prime}$ , with
> $(\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota})_{B^{\prime}}=(\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota})_{B}$ , and
> $u\in\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ such that
>
> $(T^{u}\eta)|B=x_{per}|B,\ \ but\ \ (T^{u}\eta)|H_{B}(-\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota})
> \neq x_{per}|H_{B}(-\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota}),$
>
> then there exist an $(\boldsymbol{\ell}_{J},\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota})$ -region
> $\hat{A}_{\infty}$ , with $\iota+m+1\leq J\leq\iota+2m-1$ , a configuration
> $\vartheta\in X_{\eta}$ and $\epsilon\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}$ such that
>
> (3.2) $\vartheta|\hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon)}=\hat{x}_{per}|
> \hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon)},\ \ \textrm{but}\ \ \vartheta|
> \hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon+1)}\neq\hat{x}_{per}|\hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon+1)},$
>
> where $\hat{x}_{per}:=T^{k\vec{v}_{\ell}}x_{per}$ for some $k\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}$ , for each
> $\epsilon\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}$ , [same $\hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon)}$ formula] is an
> $(\boldsymbol{\ell}_{J},\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota})$ -region and
> $0=d_{0}<d_{1}<\cdots<d_{n}<\cdots$ is the sequence where, for each
> $g\in\mathcal{H}(\boldsymbol{\ell}_{J})$ , there exists $i\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}$ such that
> $\dist(g,\boldsymbol{\ell}_{J})=d_{i}$ .

### Lemma 3.5 (proof, verbatim)

> **Proof.** We will prove item (i). The proof of item (ii) is similar. To simplify the
> notation, we will write $\boldsymbol{\ell}=\boldsymbol{\ell}_{\iota}$ . Let
> $B^{\prime}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ , with $(0,0)\in B^{\prime}$ , be an
> $E(\mathcal{S}_{\varphi})$ -enveloped such that the support line of $B^{\prime}$
> determined by $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ coincides with $\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(-)}$ and let
> $A_{0}\subset B^{\prime}$ be a non-empty set. The assumption on item (i) allows us to
> construct a sequence of $E(\mathcal{S}_{\varphi})$ -enveloped sets
>
> $B^{\prime}\subset B_{1}\subset A_{1}\subset B_{2}\subset A_{2}\subset\cdots\subset
> B_{i}\subset A_{i}\subset\cdots$
>
> such that, for each $i\in\mathbb{N}$ ,
>
> (i) $B_{i}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ is an $E(\mathcal{S}_{\varphi})$ -enveloped set with
> $B_{i}\cap\boldsymbol{\ell}_{B_{i}}\subset\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(-)}$ ,
>
> (ii) $B_{i}$ contains both $A_{i-1}$ and $[-i+1,i-1]^{2}\cap
> \mathcal{H}(\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(-)})$ ,
>
> (iii) $(T^{u_{i}}\eta)|B_{i}=x_{per}|B_{i}$ for some $u_{i}\in\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ , but
> $(T^{u_{i}}\eta)|H_{B_{i}}(\boldsymbol{\ell})\neq x_{per}|H_{B_{i}}(\boldsymbol{\ell})$ ,
>
> (iv) fixed a sequence $(u_{i})_{i\in\mathbb{N}}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ fulfilling the
> previous item, $A_{i}$ is a maximal set with respect to partial ordering by inclusion among
> all $E(\mathcal{S}_{\varphi})$ -enveloped sets $\mathcal{T}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ such that
> $B_{i}\subset\mathcal{T}\subset H_{B_{i}}(\boldsymbol{\ell})$ and
> $(T^{u_{i}}\eta)|\mathcal{T}=x_{per}|\mathcal{T}$ (see Figure 5).
>
> Let $g_{1}\in A_{1}$ denote the final point of $A_{1}\cap\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(-)}$ with
> respect to the orientation of $\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(-)}$ and, for each $i>1$ , let
> $k_{i}\in\mathbb{N}$ be such that the final point of
> $(A_{i}-k_{i}\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}})\cap\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(-)}$ with respect to the
> orientation of $\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(-)}$ coincides with $g_{1}$ . Setting
> $\hat{A}_{i}:=A_{i}-k_{i}\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}$ for all $i\in\mathbb{N}$ , where
> $k_{1}=0$ , then
>
> $(T^{k_{i}\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}+u_{i}}\eta)|\hat{A}_{i}=(T^{u_{i}}\eta)|A_{i}
> =x_{per}|A_{i}=(T^{k_{i}\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}}x_{per})|\hat{A}_{i}.$
>
> It is easy to see that each $\hat{A}_{i}$ is a maximal set among all
> $E(\mathcal{S}_{\varphi})$ -enveloped sets $\mathcal{T}\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}$ such that
>
> $B_{i}-k_{i}\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}\subset\mathcal{T}\subset
> H_{B_{i}}(\boldsymbol{\ell})-k_{i}\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}\quad\text{and}\quad
> (T^{k_{i}\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}+u_{i}}\eta)|\mathcal{T}=
> (T^{k_{i}\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}}x_{per})|\mathcal{T}.$
>
> Since $x_{per}\in X_{\eta}$ is a periodic configuration with period parallel to
> $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ , then there exists an integer $k\geq 0$ such that
> $T^{k\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}}x_{per}=T^{k_{i}\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}}x_{per}$ for
> infinitely many $i$ . By passing to a subsequence, we can assume this holds for all $i$ .
>
> Let $w_{i}(j)\in E(\hat{A}_{i})$ denote the edge of $\hat{A}_{i}$ that is parallel to the
> oriented line $\boldsymbol{\ell}_{j}$ . Since $\bigcup_{i=1}^{\infty}A_{i}=
> \mathcal{H}(\boldsymbol{\ell}^{(-)})$ , let $\iota+1\leq J\leq\iota+m-1$ be the smallest
> integer such that
>
> $\left|\hat{A}_{i}\cap w_{i}(J)\right|<\left|\hat{A}_{i+1}\cap w_{i+1}(J)\right|$
>
> for infinity many $i$ . By passing to a subsequence, we can assume that this holds for all
> $i$ . If $J>\iota+1$ , we also may assume that
>
> $|\hat{A}_{i}\cap w_{i}(j)|=|\hat{A}_{i+1}\cap w_{i+1}(j)|$
>
> for every $\iota+1\leq j\leq J-1$ and all $i$ . In particular,
> $\hat{A}_{\infty}:=\bigcup_{i=1}^{\infty}\hat{A}_{i}$ is a weakly
> $E(\mathcal{S}_{\varphi})$ -enveloped set (see Figure 6), with two semi-infinite edges, one
> of which is parallel to $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ and the other one is parallel to
> $\boldsymbol{\ell}_{J}$ . Actually, $\hat{A}_{\infty}$ is an
> $(\boldsymbol{\ell},\boldsymbol{\ell}_{J})$ -region.
>
> We define $\vartheta_{i}:=T^{k\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}+u_{i}}\eta$ for all $i$ and
> $\hat{x}_{per}:=T^{k\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}}x_{per}$ . Since
> $\vartheta_{i^{\prime}}|\hat{A}_{i}=\vartheta_{i}|\hat{A}_{i}$ for all
> $i\leq i^{\prime}$ , by compactness of $X_{\eta}$ , the sequence
> $(\vartheta_{i})_{i\in\mathbb{N}}$ has an accumulation point $\vartheta\in X_{\eta}$ such
> that $\vartheta|\hat{A}_{\infty}=\hat{x}_{per}|\hat{A}_{\infty}$ . In particular, one has
> that $\vartheta|\hat{A}_{\infty}$ is a periodic configuration with period parallel to
> $\boldsymbol{\ell}$ .
>
> We claim that there exists $\epsilon\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}$ such that
>
> $\vartheta|\hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon)}=\hat{x}_{per}|\hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon)},
> \ \ \textrm{but}\ \ \vartheta|\hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon+1)}\neq\hat{x}_{per}|
> \hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon+1)}.$
>
> Indeed, suppose, by contradiction, that
> $\vartheta|\hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon)}=\hat{x}_{per}|\hat{A}_{\infty}^{(\epsilon)}$ for
> all $\epsilon\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}$ . Note that, for an appropriate $\epsilon\in\mathbb{N}$
> fixed and all $i\in\mathbb{N}$ sufficiently large,
>
> $\hat{A}_{i}^{(\epsilon)}:=\{g-t\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}_{J+1}}\in
> \hat{A}^{(\epsilon)}_{\infty}:g\in\hat{A}_{i},\ t\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}\}$
>
> is an $E(\mathcal{S}_{\varphi})$ -enveloped set. Furthermore, one has
> $B_{i}-k_{i}\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}\subset\hat{A}_{i}^{(\epsilon)}\subset
> H_{B_{i}}(\boldsymbol{\ell})-k_{i}\vec{v}_{\boldsymbol{\ell}}$ . So let
> $\epsilon\in\mathbb{N}$ and $i_{0}\in\mathbb{N}$ be such that
> $\hat{A}_{i_{0}}^{(\epsilon)}$ is an $E(\mathcal{S}_{\varphi})$ -enveloped set and consider
> a constant $I_{0}\in\mathbb{N}$ such that
>
> $\vartheta|\hat{A}_{i_{0}}^{(\epsilon)}=\vartheta_{i}|\hat{A}_{i_{0}}^{(\epsilon)}\quad
> \forall\ i\geq I_{0}.$
>
> From our assumption it follows that $\vartheta|\hat{A}^{(\epsilon)}_{i}=
> \hat{x}_{per}|\hat{A}^{(\epsilon)}_{i}$ for all $i$ . Hence, if we consider
> $i\geq\max\{i_{0},I_{0}\}$ , then $\hat{A}_{i}^{(\epsilon)}$ is an
> $E(\mathcal{S}_{\varphi})$ -enveloped set and
> $\vartheta_{i}|\hat{A}^{(\epsilon)}_{i_{0}}=\hat{x}_{per}|\hat{A}^{(\epsilon)}_{i_{0}}$ ,
> which yields
>
> (3.3) $\vartheta_{i}|\hat{A}_{i}\cup\hat{A}^{(\epsilon)}_{i_{0}}=\hat{x}_{per}|
> \hat{A}_{i}\cup\hat{A}^{(\epsilon)}_{i_{0}}.$
>
> Since $\mathcal{S}_{\varphi}$ is $\eta$ -generating, by induction we get from (3.3) that
> $\vartheta_{i}|\hat{A}^{(\epsilon)}_{i}=\hat{x}_{per}|\hat{A}^{(\epsilon)}_{i}$ , which
> contradicts the maximality of $\hat{A}_{i}$ and concludes the proof. ∎

## What this file contains

The proof above splits into a *geometric* half (convex lattice sets, edges,
`E(S_φ)`-envelopment, the cyclic edge order, `(ℓ, ℓ_J)`-regions, the shells
`Â^{(ε)}`) and a *dynamical* half (compactness of `X_η`, the finite-orbit pigeonhole for
`T^{k_i v_ℓ} x_per`, the propagation of agreement by the generating set, and the
least-`ε` extraction).  This file proves the dynamical half **unconditionally** and
assembles Lemma 3.5 from it, taking the geometric half as *explicit hypotheses* bundled in
`Nivat.Colle35.ChainData`.

### What is proved here, with no extra hypotheses

* `exists_accumulationPoint` — compactness of `A^{ℤ²}` for a configuration sequence with
  values in a fixed finite set (the "by compactness of `X_η`" step).
* `exists_mem_orbitClosure_eqOn_iUnion` — Colle's accumulation step in one piece: a nested
  family of windows on which translates of `η` copy a fixed configuration produces
  `ϑ ∈ X_η` copying it on the whole union.
* `periodicOnWith_of_eqOn` — "In particular, `ϑ|Â_∞` is a periodic configuration with period
  parallel to `ℓ`", in the sense of Definition 2.11.
* `exists_nat_smul_mem_Per`, `exists_infinite_translate_eq` — the pigeonhole giving
  `k ≥ 0` with `T^{k v_ℓ} x_per = T^{k_i v_ℓ} x_per` for infinitely many `i`.
* `exists_shell_boundary` — the least-`ε` extraction producing (3.1) from a failure.
* `eq_at_of_generatesAt_shift`, `eqOn_of_generatesAt_of_shells` — the "since `S_φ` is
  `η`-generating, by induction we get from (3.3) …" step.
* `exists_monotone_of_infinite` — the "by passing to a subsequence" step.
* `mem_Per_T` — a period survives translation.
* the contradiction with maximality, carried out inside the proof of `lemma35`.

### What is assumed (and is *not* proved here)

The hypotheses are collected in `Nivat.Colle35.ChainData`.  Every field of that structure is
an input, not a theorem of this file.  Explicitly, the following are **assumed**:

1. `Env` — the predicate "is an `E(S_φ)`-enveloped set" is an abstract parameter.  None of
   its convex-geometric content (Definition 3.2: edges, edge counts, `|w ∩ U| ≤ |ϖ ∩ T|`)
   is formalised.
2. `B`, `A`, `u`, `kk`, `Ahat`, `AhatEq`, `AhatMono`, `envB`, `envA`, `subBA`, `subAB`,
   `subStrip`, `agreeA`, `maximalHat` — the existence of the chain
   `B' ⊂ B₁ ⊂ A₁ ⊂ B₂ ⊂ A₂ ⊂ ⋯` with properties (i)–(iv) of the proof, including the
   existence of *maximal* `E(S_φ)`-enveloped sets `A_i` (a Zorn-type input that Colle does
   not justify either), the normalising translations `k_i`, and the nestedness of the
   normalised sets `Â_i`.
3. `shell`, `shellInf`, `shellFinite`, `subShell`, `shellSubInf`, `shellInfZero`,
   `shellSubStrip`, `shellProper`, `shellEnv`, `fill`, `fillZero`, `fillStep`,
   `fillCover` — the shell families `Â_i^{(ε)}`, `Â_∞^{(ε)}`, the fact that `Â_i^{(ε)}` is
   `E(S_φ)`-enveloped for a suitable `ε` and all large `i`, the containments
   `Â_i ⊆ Â_i^{(ε)} ⊆ H_{B_i}(ℓ) - k_i v_ℓ` and `Â_i^{(ε)} ⊆ Â_∞^{(ε)}`, the fact that a
   shell of positive thickness is strictly larger than `Â_i`, and the existence of a
   filtration of `Â_i^{(ε)}` starting at `Â_i ∪ Â_{i₀}^{(ε)}` along which each new site is
   reachable by one application of the local rule of `S_φ`.
4. The classification of `Â_∞` as an `(ℓ_ι, ℓ_J)`-region with `ι+1 ≤ J ≤ ι+m-1` (the
   "smallest `J`" pigeonhole on edge lengths) is **not** formalised at all; the conclusion
   proved here is correspondingly weaker, see "Deviations" below.

### Deviations from the source, stated loudly

* **Weakened conclusion.**  Colle's conclusion asserts that `Â_∞` is an
  `(ℓ_ι, ℓ_J)`-region with `ι+1 ≤ J ≤ ι+m-1` (resp. `(ℓ_J, ℓ_ι)` with
  `ι+m+1 ≤ J ≤ ι+2m-1` in item (ii)).  `lemma35` below concludes only the *dynamical*
  part: existence of `ϑ ∈ X_η`, of `k ∈ ℤ_+`, of the set `Â_∞ = ⋃ᵢ Â_i`, and of `ε` with
  (3.1); plus `ϑ|Â_∞` periodic with period parallel to `ℓ` in the sense of Definition 2.11.
  The edge/region classification of `Â_∞` is absent.
* **Items (i) and (ii) are not distinguished.**  The direction `ℓ_ι` enters this file only
  through the lattice vector `vl`; item (ii) is item (i) applied with `vl := -v_ℓ`.  The
  file therefore proves a single theorem covering both, and the distinction between the two
  index ranges for `J` (which lives entirely in the unformalised geometry) disappears.
* **`ℝ`-normals instead of oriented lines.**  Following the convention already used in
  `Nivat/Section8/HalfPlane.lean` and the other `Nivat/External/Colle/*` files, an oriented
  line through the origin is recorded by a real normal `w`, with
  `H(ℓ) = {z | c ≤ inner2 w z}`.  Colle's `H(ℓ) = {g : ⟨g, (-u₂,u₁)⟩ ≥ 0}` is the case
  `c = 0`, `w = (-u₂, u₁)`.
* **`GeneratesAt` instead of Definition 2.2.**  The generating-set hypothesis is used
  through `Nivat.Colle.GeneratesAt` (agreement form).  `Nivat.Colle.generatesAt_iff_P_eq`
  (proved in `Nivat/External/Colle/GeneratingPatterns.lean`, deliberately not imported here
  so as to keep this file's dependency graph small) shows this is equivalent to Colle's
  pattern-count Definition 2.2 for configurations of finite range, so this is a
  reformulation, not a change of content.  Only a single generated point `gen ∈ S` is
  needed, rather than "every vertex of `S_φ` is `η`-generated".

### Non-vacuity checks performed

* `exists_accumulationPoint` and `exists_mem_orbitClosure_eqOn_iUnion` have no hypothesis
  that can fail vacuously beyond `(Set.range η).Finite`; the conclusion
  `∃ ϑ ∈ orbitClosure η, …` is never vacuous because `orbitClosure η` always contains `η`.
* `nonempty_chainData` exhibits an explicit instance of the whole hypothesis bundle
  `ChainData`, so `lemma35` is not vacuously true through a self-contradictory package.
  The bundle is genuinely constraining: `shellEnv`, `shellProper` and the agreement clause
  of `maximalHat` are jointly unsatisfiable as soon as `η` agrees with `x_per` on a whole
  shell of positive thickness, which is exactly the contradiction that drives the last
  paragraph of Colle's proof, so the witness must place a disagreement on the outer shell.
* `exists_shell_boundary` is not vacuous: its hypothesis `hfail` is *derived* inside the
  proof of `lemma35` from the maximality input, not assumed.
* The conclusion of `lemma35` is not automatically true: it asserts a *disagreement*
  (`¬ ∀ z ∈ shellInf (ε+1), …`), which no degenerate choice of `ϑ` satisfies for free.

## Status

No `sorry`.  No `axiom` is introduced by this file.
